ss I play a single card that they can beat (i.e., with a 2 or joker), they can’t play.\n\nAnd since I\'m about to play next after beating, I can choose what to play.\n\nBut if I beat with ♠2, then I must make a new play — I can play any legal combination from my hand.\n\nMy options for new play:\n- Single: ♦K, ♠6, ♥6, ♠7, ♥7\n- Pair: ♠6 ♥6, ♠7 ♥7\n\nBut teammate has only one card — so only a single would allow them to potentially play.\n\nSo if I beat with ♠2, then best to play a **small single**, like ♠6 or ♠7, so that if teammate has a higher card (unlikely), they can beat it.\n\nBut more importantly, I want to avoid helping the landlord.\n\nBest outcome: I take control, play a small single, teammate cannot beat it (probably), landlord has to respond — possibly wasting a high card.\n\nBut landlord has 3 cards — if they are holding a bomb, they might just drop it.\n\nAlternatively, if I pass, landlord keeps lead and can play freely.\n\nGiven that, better to force the landlord to react by making them respond to *my* play.\n\nTherefore, playing ♠2 is better than passing — it seizes initiative.\n\nReason: Passing would allow the landlord to maintain control with 3 cards remaining, increasing their chance to empty hand first. Playing ♠2 takes the lead, allowing me to dictate the next play. Although it uses a high card early, it is the only viable way to disrupt the landlord\'s rhythm and potentially force them to waste cards. With my teammate down to one card, gaining control may create an opportunity for them to survive longer or force the landlord into a mistake.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠2"]}'}
