at with 2.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo I should lead a single.\n\nNow: which one?\n\n- If I play a low one (♥6), landlord might also play a low-mid card (like 8 or 9) to try to win the trick cheaply.\n- Then teammate plays 2 and goes out — good.\n\n- If I play ♦K, landlord might pass (not wanting to waste a card), then teammate plays 2 and goes out — also good.\n\nEither way, if teammate has 2, they can go out.\n\nBut if landlord has a 2, they might beat the trick.\n\nFor example:\n- I play ♥6\n- Landlord plays ♦9 (from unknown)\n- Teammate plays 2 → wins, goes out → game over, peasants win.\n\nOr:\n- I play ♦K\n- Landlord has nothing higher than K except 2 or joker\n- If they have a 2, they might play it to win\n- Then they still have 2 cards left — not out yet\n- But I still have cards, teammate is out\n\nBut if landlord wins with 2, they now have 2 cards left, I have 4 cards, teammate is out — so I’m alone against landlord with more cards — risky.\n\nBetter: let teammate win the trick.\n\nSo I should play a card that the landlord is **unlikely to beat**, so that teammate can cleanly win with 2.\n\nTherefore, play a **high single** — like ♦K.\n\nBecause:\n- Landlord may not want to waste a 2 or joker on a K.\n- Especially since they have 3 cards left, they might prefer to save bombs or high cards.\n- So they are more likely to pass.\n- Then teammate plays 2 and goes out.\n\nIf I play a low card like ♥6, landlord might casually play 7, 8, 9, etc., and win the trick, preventing teammate from going out.\n\nTherefore, **best move is to play ♦K** — a high single that pressures the landlord to waste a high card or pass, giving teammate the best chance to go out.\n\nReason: Playing ♦K maximizes the chance that the landlord passes (due to its high rank), allowing my teammate — who likely holds a 2 — to play it and go out, winning the game for the peasants. Playing a lower card risks the landlord continuing with a mid-card, blocking my teammate’s exit.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦K"]}'}
